            "<strong>Prolonged labour</strong> is labour that is slower than expected — in the active phase, dilatation of less than 1 cm an hour on the classic partograph, so that the plot <strong>crosses to the right of the alert line</strong>; a prolonged latent phase is more than about 20 hours in a primigravida or 14 in a multipara; a prolonged second stage is more than 2 hours in a primigravida (3 with an epidural) or 1 hour in a multipara. The causes are the Ps: <strong>powers</strong> (inefficient or incoordinate contractions — the commonest cause in a primigravida, often with fear, dehydration, ketosis and a full bladder), <strong>passage</strong> (a small or abnormal pelvis — the adolescent, the very short woman, rickets, a previous fracture — or soft-tissue obstruction from a full bladder, fibroids or a scarred cervix) and <strong>passenger</strong> (a big baby, malposition such as occipito-posterior, malpresentation such as brow or shoulder, hydrocephalus). <strong>Obstructed labour</strong> is the end of that road: despite good contractions the presenting part <strong>cannot descend</strong> because of mechanical disproportion between it and the pelvis — <strong>cephalopelvic disproportion</strong> — and the uterus responds by contracting harder and harder, retracting its upper segment and stretching its lower segment paper-thin. The signs are a plot <strong>at or beyond the action line</strong> with <strong>no descent</strong>, <strong>moulding of ++ or +++</strong> and a large <strong>caput</strong>, a woman who is <strong>exhausted, dehydrated and febrile</strong> with a rising pulse and ketonuria, <strong>oedema of the vulva and cervix</strong>, <strong>blood-stained urine or retention</strong> (the bladder is crushed between head and symphysis), offensive liquor, fetal distress or death, and — the late and ominous sign — <strong>Bandl's ring</strong>, a <strong>visible ridge across the lower abdomen</strong> between the thick contracted upper segment and the thin overstretched lower segment, which means rupture is imminent. In a multipara the uterus ruptures; in a primigravida it is more likely to become exhausted and stop, which is why a primigravida whose contractions fade late in a long labour is not 'settling' but in trouble. The consequences of obstructed labour untreated are <strong>uterine rupture</strong>, <strong>postpartum haemorrhage</strong>, <strong>sepsis</strong>, <strong>obstetric fistula</strong> from pressure necrosis of the bladder and rectum, nerve injury with foot drop, <strong>stillbirth or asphyxia</strong>, and maternal death — the story of the woman who laboured for two days at home before reaching a district hospital with a ridge across her abdomen and a baby no longer alive.",
            "<strong>Action at the alert line</strong> (progress slower than 1 cm/h): reassess the whole picture — contractions, descent, position, moulding, bladder, hydration, fear — correct what can be corrected (empty the bladder, give oral or intravenous fluids, mobilise, support, analgesia), consider rupturing the membranes if intact and the head is well applied, and in a health centre without caesarean capability <strong>begin the referral now</strong>, because the journey takes the four hours the action line allows. <strong>Action at the action line</strong> in a hospital: a doctor assesses for disproportion; if there is none and the contractions are inadequate, labour is <strong>augmented with oxytocin</strong> with close monitoring; if there is <strong>any sign of obstruction</strong> — moulding +++, no descent, Bandl's ring, haematuria, fetal distress — augmentation is <strong>contraindicated</strong> (it ruptures the uterus) and the birth is by <strong>caesarean section</strong>, with resuscitation first: intravenous fluids, antibiotics (she is infected after a long labour), catheter and blood available, and a plan for the baby who may need resuscitation. The woman arriving exhausted and dehydrated after two days of labour at home, with a fetal head still four fifths palpable and a bladder she cannot empty, is treated as obstructed labour from the door, not re-examined for progress every four hours. A woman with obstructed labour at a health centre forty kilometres from the hospital is transferred with an intravenous line running, a catheter, antibiotics given, the <strong>oxytocin — if any was started — stopped</strong>, a midwife beside her, the partograph in the folder and a telephone call ahead; she is not kept 'to see whether she progresses'.",
