            "<strong>Induction</strong> is starting labour artificially; <strong>augmentation</strong> is strengthening a labour that has started but is inadequate. Induction is offered when continuing the pregnancy is more dangerous than birth and vaginal birth is feasible: <strong>prolonged pregnancy</strong> (from 41 weeks), <strong>prelabour rupture of the membranes</strong> at term without labour after about 24 hours, <strong>pre-eclampsia</strong> and other maternal disease once stable, <strong>intrauterine death</strong>, <strong>fetal growth restriction</strong> and diabetes at term, and chorioamnionitis. It is <strong>contraindicated</strong> when vaginal birth itself is unsafe — placenta praevia, transverse lie, cord presentation, previous classical caesarean or uterine rupture, severe fetal distress, active genital herpes — and is approached with caution after a previous lower-segment caesarean (prostaglandins are avoided; a Foley catheter and careful oxytocin may be used in hospital). The <strong>Bishop score</strong> (dilatation, effacement, consistency and position of the cervix, and station of the head, each scored 0–3) predicts success: a <strong>score of 6 or less is unfavourable</strong> and needs cervical ripening first; a favourable cervix (7 or more) can go straight to amniotomy and oxytocin.",
            "<strong>Cervical ripening</strong>: <strong>misoprostol 25 micrograms vaginally every 6 hours</strong> (or 25 micrograms orally every 2 hours as a solution) — <em>low</em> doses, never the 200-microgram tablet whole for a live fetus — up to a maximum number of doses, with the fetal heart and contractions checked before each; a <strong>transcervical Foley catheter</strong> (balloon inflated with 30–60 mL above the internal os) ripens mechanically with less hyperstimulation and is the method of choice after a previous caesarean; dinoprostone where available. Oxytocin is not started within 4–6 hours of the last misoprostol dose. <strong>Oxytocin</strong> for induction or augmentation is given by <strong>controlled infusion</strong> — in Ghana commonly 10 IU in 1,000 mL (or 5 IU in 500 mL) of normal saline or Ringer's lactate — <strong>starting at a low rate (1–2 milliunits per minute) and doubling every 30 minutes</strong> until <strong>three to four contractions in ten minutes lasting 40–60 seconds</strong>, then held there, never exceeding the maximum in the protocol; the aim is adequate, not maximal, contractions. The nurse's responsibilities are exact: the fetal heart and contractions every 15–30 minutes (continuous CTG where available), the infusion rate and the response written on the partograph, the <strong>recognition of hyperstimulation</strong> — more than five contractions in ten minutes, a contraction lasting over 60–90 seconds (the woman on an augmentation infusion whose contractions last 100 seconds), a rise in resting tone, or fetal distress — and the response: <strong>stop the infusion</strong>, turn her to the left, give fluids, call the doctor and consider terbutaline; the recognition of <strong>water intoxication</strong> (oxytocin is an antidiuretic — confusion, headache, vomiting, convulsions after prolonged high-dose infusion in large volumes of dextrose: stop it, check sodium, use saline); the protection of a <strong>scarred uterus</strong> (oxytocin after a previous caesarean is a doctor's decision with continuous monitoring and a low threshold to stop); and the rule that <strong>oxytocin is never used when there is obstruction or a malpresentation that cannot deliver vaginally</strong>. Augmentation in a multipara needs extra caution — her uterus ruptures more easily — and a labour that has stopped progressing despite adequate contractions is disproportion until proven otherwise.",
